1
00:00:16,057 --> 00:00:16,698
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the aim?

2
00:00:17,058 --> 00:00:17,818
[SPEAKER_00]: What is your aim?

3
00:00:19,759 --> 00:00:24,801
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the response is usually, well, freedom from suffering.

4
00:00:24,961 --> 00:00:29,803
[SPEAKER_00]: But what do you mean by suffering that you're trying to be free from?

5
00:00:30,263 --> 00:00:32,585
[SPEAKER_00]: And what would freedom mean?

6
00:00:34,106 --> 00:00:34,286
[SPEAKER_04]: Right.

7
00:00:34,406 --> 00:00:34,747
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, yeah.

8
00:00:34,787 --> 00:00:36,708
[SPEAKER_04]: So, what is suffering, basically?

9
00:00:37,489 --> 00:00:37,689
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

10
00:00:37,889 --> 00:00:39,731
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you understand by... what are you trying to escape?

11
00:00:39,831 --> 00:00:40,151
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

12
00:00:41,352 --> 00:00:47,317
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, eh... the reason a mind suffers is because the mind doesn't understand suffering.

13
00:00:47,717 --> 00:00:47,817
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

14
00:00:47,837 --> 00:00:50,019
[SPEAKER_04]: Not because mind experiences suffering.

15
00:00:51,120 --> 00:00:54,503
[SPEAKER_04]: But, of course, everybody starts by non-understanding, so...

16
00:00:55,536 --> 00:01:03,678
[SPEAKER_04]: Firstly, you experience suffering, you want to get rid of it, you want to deal with it, you want to seek, you want to practice, you want to just do something about it.

17
00:01:03,918 --> 00:01:18,981
[SPEAKER_04]: But then, yes, if you have come across the right instruction, then you will... you should be realizing gradually that, right, that which I'm trying to get rid of, that suffering, that pain, actually needs to be understood in order to get rid of it.

18
00:01:20,401 --> 00:01:22,482
[SPEAKER_04]: So then, do I understand suffering?

19
00:01:22,502 --> 00:01:23,482
[SPEAKER_04]: What is suffering for me?

20
00:01:25,253 --> 00:01:29,154
[SPEAKER_04]: So, when I experience discomfort, what is... where is that suffering?

21
00:01:29,314 --> 00:01:35,476
[SPEAKER_05]: Is it just state of mind, state of the feeling, both, neither, in between?

22
00:01:35,596 --> 00:01:38,377
[SPEAKER_05]: Have I understood what suffering is?

23
00:01:38,397 --> 00:01:46,939
[SPEAKER_04]: Because I'm, you know, I feel justified in being preoccupied and constantly seeking the answer and practice to get rid of it.

24
00:01:47,511 --> 00:01:53,437
[SPEAKER_04]: But as I said, if you actually start reading the suttas closely, you realize, getting rid means understanding.

25
00:01:53,937 --> 00:01:55,299
[SPEAKER_04]: That's how you abandon it.

26
00:01:56,160 --> 00:01:57,541
[SPEAKER_04]: Not, not, not having it.

27
00:01:59,563 --> 00:02:01,545
[SPEAKER_04]: So, what is there that needs to be understood?

28
00:02:06,630 --> 00:02:07,351
[SPEAKER_04]: So, what is suffering?

29
00:02:07,371 --> 00:02:07,531
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

30
00:02:07,911 --> 00:02:08,512
[SPEAKER_04]: Let's start with that.

31
00:02:15,480 --> 00:02:18,361
[SPEAKER_01]: Mental anguish, pressure.

32
00:02:19,401 --> 00:02:19,841
[SPEAKER_03]: Pressure.

33
00:02:20,861 --> 00:02:24,442
[SPEAKER_01]: But in terms of anguish and stress, is it?

34
00:02:24,783 --> 00:02:25,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Stress.

35
00:02:25,263 --> 00:02:26,043
[SPEAKER_01]: Mental stress.

36
00:02:26,583 --> 00:02:35,825
[SPEAKER_04]: So, can you have a pleasant feeling, experienced through your senses and so on, but mentally suffer on account of it?

37
00:02:37,286 --> 00:02:41,627
[SPEAKER_04]: Can you have an unpleasant feeling, but mentally not suffer on account of it?

38
00:02:44,535 --> 00:02:45,476
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes, to all.

39
00:02:45,636 --> 00:02:46,436
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly, yeah.

40
00:02:47,077 --> 00:02:57,285
[SPEAKER_04]: So, so then, suffering is not in how you feel, which is why we come back to what I keep saying on and on and on and on.

41
00:02:58,245 --> 00:03:04,811
[SPEAKER_04]: You have to stop feeling, taking for granted and feeling justified in trying to get rid of your feelings, because they bother you.

42
00:03:04,831 --> 00:03:13,277
[SPEAKER_04]: Because you're acting out of the bother of the feeling, means you're affirming your assumption, your wrong view, that the problem is in the feeling.

43
00:03:14,997 --> 00:03:16,438
[SPEAKER_04]: So, where is then that suffering?

44
00:03:17,058 --> 00:03:30,206
[SPEAKER_04]: If it's not in the feeling, if... if you can sometimes feel good or bad, but not necessarily suffer... See, if the suffering is in the feeling, each time you're experiencing discomfort or feeling not so good, you would also have to suffer on account of it.

45
00:03:30,246 --> 00:03:43,233
[SPEAKER_04]: But the fact that you don't, means it does not actually match, doesn't add up, that suffering is there, because otherwise, why are you not suffering?

46
00:03:47,050 --> 00:03:49,571
[SPEAKER_02]: Aren't there two types of unpleasant feelings?

47
00:03:49,952 --> 00:04:00,897
[SPEAKER_02]: So, some are connected with, let's say, bodily state, I don't know, whatever, and others are connected with, let's say, well, I'd call them your karma, basically.

48
00:04:01,657 --> 00:04:09,821
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a result of your unwholesome actions that cause, sort of, distress, maybe remorse, and all this, you see?

49
00:04:10,381 --> 00:04:11,002
[SPEAKER_02]: All the hindrances.

50
00:04:12,065 --> 00:04:13,045
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, yeah, but that's irrelevant.

51
00:04:14,026 --> 00:04:17,648
[SPEAKER_04]: You can have two types, six types, neither of them is the cause of suffering.

52
00:04:18,388 --> 00:04:23,710
[SPEAKER_04]: Because if your karma would be the cause of suffering, means you wouldn't be able to do anything about it until the karma runs out.

53
00:04:24,651 --> 00:04:26,972
[SPEAKER_04]: And as the Buddha said himself, that's a completely wrong view.

54
00:04:27,232 --> 00:04:28,313
[SPEAKER_04]: It's a form of determinism.

55
00:04:29,293 --> 00:04:37,257
[SPEAKER_04]: So, yes, some might be caused by karma, some might be caused by what you did, what you thought, some might be caused by immediate physical experience, some might be caused by change of elements.

56
00:04:38,519 --> 00:04:43,563
[SPEAKER_04]: There's just why they're there, but that doesn't matter, because none of them is the root of suffering.

57
00:04:45,424 --> 00:04:46,405
[SPEAKER_04]: Presently, there isn't cause.

58
00:04:46,685 --> 00:04:47,645
[SPEAKER_04]: Presently, there isn't suffering.

59
00:04:48,786 --> 00:04:49,627
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's exactly the point.

60
00:04:49,647 --> 00:04:58,113
[SPEAKER_04]: See, people would be concerned about where their feelings came from and why, because they still assume that the suffering is in them or defined by them.

61
00:05:03,804 --> 00:05:15,251
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's the... in the broadest terms, as we said so many times before, it's your presently enduring attitude to the presently enduring feeling, in regard to the presently enduring feeling.

62
00:05:15,712 --> 00:05:20,235
[SPEAKER_04]: That's where the suffering is.

63
00:05:20,255 --> 00:05:20,935
[SPEAKER_04]: So... uh-huh?

64
00:05:21,335 --> 00:05:30,081
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it possible to be, say, I know, enlightened and still suffer on account of your hindrances, let's say?

65
00:05:31,408 --> 00:05:31,648
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

66
00:05:32,369 --> 00:05:37,952
[SPEAKER_04]: It's possible to be enlightened and still have discomfort and uncomfortable feelings coming.

67
00:05:38,652 --> 00:05:39,012
[SPEAKER_04]: That's true.

68
00:05:39,292 --> 00:05:40,533
[SPEAKER_04]: But you won't suffer and encounter them.

69
00:05:42,254 --> 00:05:50,158
[SPEAKER_02]: But isn't it like this, that the nature of these feelings, so, the origin of these feelings is changed.

70
00:05:50,419 --> 00:05:56,622
[SPEAKER_02]: So, they might be coming from body, but they're not going to come from the hindrances.

71
00:05:59,242 --> 00:05:59,522
[SPEAKER_04]: No, no.

72
00:05:59,622 --> 00:06:00,822
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, it doesn't matter.

73
00:06:01,122 --> 00:06:01,682
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the point.

74
00:06:01,802 --> 00:06:15,746
[SPEAKER_04]: Like in that sutta where the Buddha lists those seven or eight things, why, because of which one might feel suffering, he was just to tell you, basically, that one of the reasons was circumstances, an accident.

75
00:06:16,506 --> 00:06:17,787
[SPEAKER_01]: Why one might feel?

76
00:06:18,645 --> 00:06:20,226
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, one might experience discomfort.

77
00:06:20,426 --> 00:06:20,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling.

78
00:06:20,726 --> 00:06:21,947
[SPEAKER_04]: Feeling, yeah, sorry, not suffering.

79
00:06:22,087 --> 00:06:26,229
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, why one might feel discomfort and experience uncomfortable feelings?

80
00:06:26,829 --> 00:06:29,430
[SPEAKER_04]: Change of the elements, change of the bile and so on.

81
00:06:29,810 --> 00:06:31,791
[SPEAKER_04]: Previous karma was just one of the reasons.

82
00:06:32,752 --> 00:06:44,857
[SPEAKER_04]: In other words, the whole point was like, yes, they will have a reason to be there, the reasons might change, but if feeling was not a problem to start with, why it is there and what reasons are behind it are equally not a problem.

83
00:06:45,658 --> 00:06:46,698
[SPEAKER_04]: And feeling is not a problem.

84
00:06:47,450 --> 00:06:49,070
[SPEAKER_04]: The root of suffering is not feeling.

85
00:06:50,190 --> 00:06:52,171
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not one suffers because one feels.

86
00:06:53,731 --> 00:06:53,891
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

87
00:06:54,031 --> 00:06:55,971
[SPEAKER_04]: One suffers because one craves.

88
00:06:58,732 --> 00:07:05,413
[SPEAKER_04]: And one craves in regard to what one feels, regardless of why one feels that or this, and where it came from.

89
00:07:06,813 --> 00:07:08,714
[SPEAKER_04]: So, present enduring attitude, i.e.

90
00:07:08,794 --> 00:07:16,775
[SPEAKER_04]: craving, towards present enduring feeling, regardless of why it came, and why, and for how long, and what caused it, and whether it will be caused again,

91
00:07:23,357 --> 00:07:24,318
[SPEAKER_04]: So, how do you start?

92
00:07:25,798 --> 00:07:36,144
[SPEAKER_04]: By upgrading, or removing the wrong attitude, or fading away, or abandoning, relinquishing, overcoming the wrong attitude towards whatever you're currently feeling.

93
00:07:36,244 --> 00:07:38,125
[SPEAKER_04]: What would be the necessary first step?

94
00:07:39,446 --> 00:07:40,687
[SPEAKER_03]: Sense restraint and virtue.

95
00:07:41,347 --> 00:07:41,708
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

96
00:07:41,848 --> 00:07:42,508
[SPEAKER_04]: Why?

97
00:07:42,528 --> 00:07:43,328
[SPEAKER_04]: Because it's non...

98
00:07:44,849 --> 00:07:52,113
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, it's emulating non-craving to...

99
00:07:53,972 --> 00:07:55,352
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, instead of...

100
00:07:55,412 --> 00:08:01,454
[SPEAKER_04]: In other words, because nonsense restraint and non-virtue would always be rooted in craving.

101
00:08:01,574 --> 00:08:02,935
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

102
00:08:02,975 --> 00:08:03,895
[SPEAKER_04]: Whether you know it or not.

103
00:08:03,995 --> 00:08:05,095
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, it requires craving.

104
00:08:05,696 --> 00:08:10,257
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, and that craving would then perpetuate the assumptions where the problem is.

105
00:08:11,077 --> 00:08:19,100
[SPEAKER_04]: Acting out of craving feeds the assumptions as to what you should act towards and where the problem is.

106
00:08:19,120 --> 00:08:19,360
[SPEAKER_05]: So,

107
00:08:21,375 --> 00:08:37,162
[SPEAKER_04]: Virtue, sense restraint, and as we discussed in previous talks, that kind of starts revealing the the intentions, the choices on that mental level, makes it more obvious, which is what starts constituting the development of the mind.

108
00:08:38,502 --> 00:08:50,988
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if you're not acting by body, by speech, you're now discerning the intentions, the leaning, the attitudes of the mind in regard to this that's felt, or that that is felt, you can start discerning the difference, or at least distinguishing it.

109
00:08:51,927 --> 00:08:57,111
[SPEAKER_04]: Now, you can't willingly choose to not have a certain attitude towards a feeling.

110
00:08:57,531 --> 00:09:08,699
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if there is a prospect of agreeable feeling, sensuality, safety of some sorts, your mind is delighting in it.

111
00:09:08,919 --> 00:09:09,720
[SPEAKER_04]: So, what do you do?

112
00:09:11,281 --> 00:09:13,923
[SPEAKER_04]: Do you get rid of the delight by denying the thoughts?

113
00:09:14,183 --> 00:09:14,623
[SPEAKER_04]: What do you do?

114
00:09:14,964 --> 00:09:18,546
[SPEAKER_04]: How do you not delight when the Buddha says he doesn't delight in that feeling?

115
00:09:19,067 --> 00:09:19,987
[SPEAKER_04]: He doesn't welcome it.

116
00:09:20,307 --> 00:09:20,948
[SPEAKER_04]: How do you do that?

117
00:09:23,713 --> 00:09:24,574
[SPEAKER_04]: How can you do that?

118
00:09:24,694 --> 00:09:27,936
[SPEAKER_04]: Can you just choose to not delight in what the mind is already delighting in?

119
00:09:29,637 --> 00:09:32,959
[SPEAKER_04]: How did the Buddha do that while he was still a bodhisattva, as he said in that sutta?

120
00:09:33,840 --> 00:09:34,761
[SPEAKER_04]: Contemplating danger.

121
00:09:35,421 --> 00:09:36,141
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

122
00:09:36,202 --> 00:09:40,625
[SPEAKER_04]: Contemplating the context, the wisdom that you got on account of pondering and reflecting.

123
00:09:41,125 --> 00:09:43,867
[SPEAKER_04]: So, my mind is delighting in the prospect of this.

124
00:09:44,007 --> 00:09:44,787
[SPEAKER_04]: I can't stop that.

125
00:09:44,887 --> 00:09:48,510
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, what I can stop is acting out automatically, habitually.

126
00:09:49,070 --> 00:09:51,152
[SPEAKER_04]: And what certainly I can provide for this

127
00:09:52,365 --> 00:09:58,194
[SPEAKER_04]: untamed mind, this beast that wants to go and feed in other people's pastures, well, I can keep just

128
00:09:59,673 --> 00:10:06,576
[SPEAKER_04]: Talking to it, in a sense of providing the context, the danger, reminders, and it will have to listen.

129
00:10:06,716 --> 00:10:07,596
[SPEAKER_04]: It cannot ignore it.

130
00:10:08,076 --> 00:10:22,722
[SPEAKER_04]: It might not listen instantaneously, but as the Buddha said, he would think about the peril, the lack of satisfaction, how this is not beneficial for me or for others, and dwelling on that context, while the mind delights the sensuality, you would stop delighting in it.

131
00:10:23,343 --> 00:10:24,343
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you go rid of it.

132
00:10:25,404 --> 00:10:28,825
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, ill will comes, thoughts of cruelty come, same.

133
00:10:30,636 --> 00:10:38,047
[SPEAKER_04]: You work on the sustaining the context, composing the context, the samādhi of the context of... No, this is harmful, factually harmful.

134
00:10:38,107 --> 00:10:40,190
[SPEAKER_04]: You don't need to be... Is this harmful?

135
00:10:40,331 --> 00:10:42,233
[SPEAKER_04]: Let me look at the etymology of the word harmful.

136
00:10:42,374 --> 00:10:43,195
[SPEAKER_04]: You know it's harmful.

137
00:10:45,948 --> 00:10:52,873
[SPEAKER_04]: Results in further engagement, further hassle, further proliferation, further repercussions for you, for others, for both.

138
00:10:53,554 --> 00:10:57,317
[SPEAKER_04]: And every time that you can remember in the past, it always resulted in the same.

139
00:10:57,477 --> 00:11:00,900
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's why even you want to practice now, because on some level you already know

140
00:11:01,440 --> 00:11:03,041
[SPEAKER_04]: It is not beneficial for me.

141
00:11:03,662 --> 00:11:22,353
[SPEAKER_04]: So, hammer that in, and keep hammering that in, day and night, not blindly, like some sort of mantra, but when the mind delights towards sensuality, when the mind leans, has the attitude towards cruelty, or thoughts of ill-will, and so on, you provide a context, so the mind comes down.

142
00:11:22,573 --> 00:11:24,274
[SPEAKER_04]: You can't just tell the mind to come down.

143
00:11:24,334 --> 00:11:25,475
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, eventually you can.

144
00:11:27,454 --> 00:11:30,936
[SPEAKER_04]: When you tamed it, then it becomes pliable and listens to you.

145
00:11:31,816 --> 00:11:40,981
[SPEAKER_04]: And then you could directly go over there, or go over there, or investigate this, don't think about that, and it will listen every single time, because you did the work of taming it down first.

146
00:11:42,302 --> 00:11:47,305
[SPEAKER_04]: But even then, it's not a matter of the choice that you think choice is now, the way it is.

147
00:11:47,385 --> 00:11:51,247
[SPEAKER_04]: It's a different relationship, but yes, you have developed it.

148
00:11:52,392 --> 00:12:04,601
[SPEAKER_03]: Whereas if you're used to acting out of it, going towards the content, then asking these questions just is harder and harder, and it's less and less... Yeah, they become more and more abstract, and they drift away.

149
00:12:04,661 --> 00:12:12,486
[SPEAKER_04]: So that's why, like, first you have to stop engaging with it physically, verbally, you know, bite your tongue if you have to, and not speak, and not act.

150
00:12:13,827 --> 00:12:17,170
[SPEAKER_04]: Again, not just out of some mystical release, David.

151
00:12:17,350 --> 00:12:20,272
[SPEAKER_04]: So that you can basically start discerning, oh,

152
00:12:20,948 --> 00:12:29,755
[SPEAKER_04]: The context, the mind, the intentions, let me ponder on these thoughts, these dangers, within what mind is delighting, or within what mind is resisting.

153
00:12:29,876 --> 00:12:33,779
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like this, in mundane sense, the intervention, you know.

154
00:12:34,620 --> 00:12:44,067
[SPEAKER_01]: This person is addicted to something, and then the family gets around and intervenes, you know, and tells him, look what you're doing, look what is happening.

155
00:12:44,888 --> 00:12:54,697
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, maybe for a while, he gets the picture, gets the context, goes to rehab or whatever, but with yourself, you have to... that's what you're going to do with your mind.

156
00:12:54,837 --> 00:12:55,017
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

157
00:12:55,157 --> 00:12:56,198
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, always intervene.

158
00:12:56,258 --> 00:12:59,762
[SPEAKER_04]: And the reason why it's a good example also, it's because it is kind of indirect.

159
00:13:00,262 --> 00:13:07,407
[SPEAKER_04]: See, even in mundane sense, the intervention is a successful one when a person starts, decides on account of what you've been telling him.

160
00:13:07,827 --> 00:13:10,489
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not a really successful one when you just force them to.

161
00:13:10,749 --> 00:13:14,611
[SPEAKER_04]: They might do it for as long as there is a force applied.

162
00:13:14,631 --> 00:13:17,753
[SPEAKER_04]: The moment that stops, they drift back because the views haven't changed.

163
00:13:17,833 --> 00:13:20,395
[SPEAKER_03]: Understanding is when you've seen the harm.

164
00:13:20,415 --> 00:13:20,795
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

165
00:13:20,955 --> 00:13:22,216
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you can't...

166
00:13:22,236 --> 00:13:23,677
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, you can try and force your mind.

167
00:13:24,258 --> 00:13:25,959
[SPEAKER_04]: But you can see what it will lead to.

168
00:13:26,559 --> 00:13:27,739
[SPEAKER_04]: The point is, no, no, no.

169
00:13:27,819 --> 00:13:30,900
[SPEAKER_04]: You prevent it from making it further, worse, further.

170
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:35,282
[SPEAKER_04]: You prevent it from kind of aggravating the situation through, like, non-restraint and non-virtue.

171
00:13:35,802 --> 00:13:44,985
[SPEAKER_04]: Then, your work is to just feed it this context, and sustain the context, and protect the context, and make sure you don't forget or choose to willingly distract yourself from the context.

172
00:13:45,745 --> 00:13:48,986
[SPEAKER_04]: And the mind will have to hear it, because it's just... that's all it sees now.

173
00:13:49,546 --> 00:13:50,747
[SPEAKER_04]: Like that taming the elephant.

174
00:13:50,767 --> 00:13:57,269
[SPEAKER_04]: You first have to get it out of the wild forest that's... that's... that's sort of perpetuating its wild behavior.

175
00:13:57,549 --> 00:13:59,430
[SPEAKER_04]: And then it will kick and scream when it comes down.

176
00:13:59,470 --> 00:14:00,750
[SPEAKER_04]: Then, okay, well, here's the food.

177
00:14:01,231 --> 00:14:01,651
[SPEAKER_04]: Here is it.

178
00:14:01,751 --> 00:14:04,532
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, it starts taking the food, starts listening, starts recognizing me.

179
00:14:04,992 --> 00:14:07,033
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, now stand up, move down, do this, do this.

180
00:14:07,413 --> 00:14:13,695
[SPEAKER_04]: All of that, it's on the level of making the mind turn away from things that are unbeneficial for the mind and for you.

181
00:14:15,057 --> 00:14:16,138
[SPEAKER_04]: But you can't force it.

182
00:14:16,639 --> 00:14:20,643
[SPEAKER_04]: You can't magically have something that will just result in this... Oh, how did I get there?

183
00:14:20,723 --> 00:14:21,523
[SPEAKER_04]: Anyway, it doesn't matter.

184
00:14:21,543 --> 00:14:22,745
[SPEAKER_04]: This must be what nibbāna is.

185
00:14:23,445 --> 00:14:23,605
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

186
00:14:23,966 --> 00:14:25,948
[SPEAKER_04]: You train it away from it.

187
00:14:26,987 --> 00:14:27,727
[SPEAKER_04]: Gradual training.

188
00:14:28,088 --> 00:14:29,068
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, it will listen to you.

189
00:14:29,108 --> 00:14:40,054
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, if the mind listens to you, through perpetually feeding the context, sustaining the context, whether you're sitting, walking, going to the toilet, or whatever else, then, you won't have to feed that context as much.

190
00:14:40,194 --> 00:14:41,194
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's exactly what Buddha said.

191
00:14:41,254 --> 00:14:41,554
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, wait.

192
00:14:42,155 --> 00:14:43,916
[SPEAKER_04]: Now, my mind is not going back to these things.

193
00:14:43,956 --> 00:14:45,076
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm not getting these thoughts.

194
00:14:45,356 --> 00:14:46,997
[SPEAKER_04]: It doesn't delight in sensuality anymore.

195
00:14:47,017 --> 00:14:47,938
[SPEAKER_04]: There is no more cruelty.

196
00:14:48,738 --> 00:14:49,658
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, I could rest now.

197
00:14:50,039 --> 00:14:53,880
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't need to be constantly working on the context, you know, the two types of thought.

198
00:14:54,461 --> 00:14:57,182
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, so let me now steady this mind internally.

199
00:14:57,462 --> 00:14:58,282
[SPEAKER_04]: No work to be done.

200
00:14:58,682 --> 00:15:02,024
[SPEAKER_04]: If sensuality comes coming back again, or it will, well, okay, I know what to do.

201
00:15:02,164 --> 00:15:04,665
[SPEAKER_04]: The context, bring it back until the mind calms down.

202
00:15:05,786 --> 00:15:07,326
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's what led into first jhāna.

203
00:15:08,887 --> 00:15:13,149
[SPEAKER_04]: Calming down within the right context of thinking and pondering that the mind has withdrawn from.

204
00:15:18,628 --> 00:15:21,852
[SPEAKER_05]: And the context is basically understanding beneficial and unbeneficial.

205
00:15:22,072 --> 00:15:25,996
[SPEAKER_04]: The context is always in regard to where your mind is leaning.

206
00:15:26,457 --> 00:15:29,700
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not a universal sort of thing that's, oh, context, context.

207
00:15:29,720 --> 00:15:29,940
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

208
00:15:30,581 --> 00:15:32,323
[SPEAKER_04]: First, what's the state of the mind now?

209
00:15:32,383 --> 00:15:34,045
[SPEAKER_04]: What are you currently feeling?

210
00:15:34,505 --> 00:15:36,447
[SPEAKER_04]: What is your attitude towards what you're currently feeling?

211
00:15:37,028 --> 00:15:37,749
[SPEAKER_04]: Are you delighting?

212
00:15:37,769 --> 00:15:38,449
[SPEAKER_04]: Are you resisting?

213
00:15:38,469 --> 00:15:39,130
[SPEAKER_04]: Are you fearing it?

214
00:15:39,690 --> 00:15:42,092
[SPEAKER_04]: Context in regard to that, every single time.

215
00:15:42,652 --> 00:15:46,115
[SPEAKER_04]: Whenever there is a feeling present, you gotta work on the context in regard to it.

216
00:15:46,575 --> 00:15:48,877
[SPEAKER_04]: Otherwise, you'll be conflating feeling for your suffering.

217
00:15:50,164 --> 00:16:03,832
[SPEAKER_03]: Whereas, if you're acting out of sensuality or will, distraction, you're... what is in front of you, you cannot establish the context, because you're always changing what's in front of you.

218
00:16:04,012 --> 00:16:04,752
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

219
00:16:04,933 --> 00:16:12,337
[SPEAKER_04]: Or at least hoping that it will result in changing what's in front of me, which just perpetuates the view that you think the problem was there in front of you.

220
00:16:12,597 --> 00:16:20,881
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you're not allowing that thing to endure, to then be able to establish... To reveal your attitude of, like, whether the mind is delighting or resisting and so on.

221
00:16:21,161 --> 00:16:24,702
[SPEAKER_04]: You won't see the signs of your mind if you don't let things endure on that mental level.

222
00:16:24,882 --> 00:16:25,123
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

223
00:16:28,844 --> 00:16:31,986
[SPEAKER_02]: Are there any means how one can accelerate this process?

224
00:16:32,026 --> 00:16:37,548
[SPEAKER_04]: So... Well, probably the first mean would be the question you need to ask yourself.

225
00:16:37,588 --> 00:16:39,389
[SPEAKER_04]: Why would you want to accelerate it?

226
00:16:40,070 --> 00:16:50,953
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I mean, it's obvious to say we have finite life and it's better whether you believe... Yeah, but no, what makes you think that already the practice is not fast enough or something?

227
00:16:51,033 --> 00:16:52,413
[SPEAKER_04]: Why do you want it to be even faster?

228
00:16:52,933 --> 00:16:55,394
[SPEAKER_04]: Is it because it's not happening?

229
00:16:55,414 --> 00:16:56,354
[SPEAKER_04]: Or because you still suffer?

230
00:16:57,595 --> 00:17:03,776
[SPEAKER_04]: So, in a way, yes, if you understand suffering thoroughly right here and now, you'll be an arahant right here and now.

231
00:17:04,517 --> 00:17:09,658
[SPEAKER_04]: So, means of accelerating the process are not on the level of process.

232
00:17:10,945 --> 00:17:12,208
[SPEAKER_04]: They're on the level of understanding.

233
00:17:13,530 --> 00:17:18,881
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if you're coming from a point of view of a method, are there things that I can do to speed this up?

234
00:17:19,402 --> 00:17:19,543
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

235
00:17:20,485 --> 00:17:26,168
[SPEAKER_04]: If you're coming from a point of view of determination to understand clarity and protecting the context, yes, and that's exactly what you do.

236
00:17:26,829 --> 00:17:29,350
[SPEAKER_04]: But even then, you can't say how fast it will be.

237
00:17:29,430 --> 00:17:31,111
[SPEAKER_04]: The Buddha couldn't say how fast it would be.

238
00:17:31,772 --> 00:17:45,620
[SPEAKER_04]: He said, if somebody practices, upon their right view, they practice four foundations of mindfulness correctly, well, it might take seven days, seven weeks, seven months, seven years, or what, half a night, if they do it rightly.

239
00:17:45,660 --> 00:17:48,702
[SPEAKER_04]: In the same sense, he gave the simile of the carpenter with an axe.

240
00:17:49,828 --> 00:17:55,173
[SPEAKER_04]: He uses it, he works on it, he's not concerned about how quickly the handle is wearing thin.

241
00:17:55,993 --> 00:17:59,256
[SPEAKER_04]: But he knows it has to wear thin, because I'm using it.

242
00:18:00,117 --> 00:18:05,662
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if your concern is on the acceleration, means you're kind of not seeing it on the level of understanding.

243
00:18:06,403 --> 00:18:12,428
[SPEAKER_04]: If you're seeing it on the level of understanding, consideration wouldn't bother you, because you wouldn't have craving in regard to the speed of your freedom from craving.

244
00:18:14,500 --> 00:18:34,930
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there was this example from, of Venerable Ananda, who could not, well, wasn't enlightened, or wasn't an arahant, and he was trying to accelerate it, basically, and he was even reproached that, instead of meditating, he was sort of soothing the people's

245
00:18:35,970 --> 00:18:37,711
[SPEAKER_02]: pain because of the demise of the Buddha.

246
00:18:37,991 --> 00:18:49,898
[SPEAKER_02]: So, so apparently, or would you say that if he didn't meditate, then he was wasn't really sustaining this attitude, so that he needed to really to sit in order to to make progress?

247
00:18:50,573 --> 00:18:51,334
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't quite understand.

248
00:18:51,354 --> 00:18:53,195
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you say it again?

249
00:18:53,535 --> 00:19:01,580
[SPEAKER_02]: So, apparently, he, through his meditation and through his sitting, he managed to accelerate it to the point that he attained...

250
00:19:01,640 --> 00:19:02,540
[SPEAKER_02]: Right, right, right, right.

251
00:19:02,580 --> 00:19:06,743
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, again, relatively speaking, it wasn't the meditation or the sitting.

252
00:19:07,043 --> 00:19:07,904
[SPEAKER_04]: He was actually walking.

253
00:19:08,824 --> 00:19:10,865
[SPEAKER_04]: And it was... that did not accelerate it.

254
00:19:11,426 --> 00:19:13,987
[SPEAKER_04]: It was what made him do that, accelerate it.

255
00:19:14,087 --> 00:19:19,411
[SPEAKER_04]: And what made him do that is the fact that he, closest to the Buddha,

256
00:19:19,991 --> 00:19:25,138
[SPEAKER_04]: He was not allowed now to join the meeting of arahants, because he's still a trainee.

257
00:19:25,639 --> 00:19:32,308
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it was a sense of shame, lack of accomplishment, weakness, that made him then make the effort.

258
00:19:34,375 --> 00:19:39,200
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, when he finished walking meditation and contemplation, that's what walking meditation is.

259
00:19:39,360 --> 00:19:40,781
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not doing... make...

260
00:19:41,021 --> 00:19:41,802
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, how could it?

261
00:19:41,882 --> 00:19:46,867
[SPEAKER_04]: How could you watching soles of your feet, for example, where people generally would think, oh, slow movement.

262
00:19:46,907 --> 00:19:50,130
[SPEAKER_04]: How can that reveal the context of the actual, current, enduring feeling?

263
00:19:50,150 --> 00:19:50,270
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

264
00:19:50,531 --> 00:19:52,312
[SPEAKER_04]: Only you investigate in that direction.

265
00:19:52,713 --> 00:19:53,433
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's what he did.

266
00:19:53,914 --> 00:19:57,858
[SPEAKER_04]: He protected the context in regard to any thoughts that had arisen, that was of the unwholesome nature.

267
00:19:58,478 --> 00:20:08,348
[SPEAKER_04]: He established his mind upon the nature of an enduring body, for example, such as that it's unownable to the point of the soles of... top of your head to the soles of your feet, and he drilled that throughout the night.

268
00:20:08,708 --> 00:20:15,115
[SPEAKER_04]: He protected the context, he steadied his mind in it, he did not get distracted from it, when he said, okay, I did what I could do.

269
00:20:16,596 --> 00:20:17,838
[SPEAKER_04]: I have to lay down and rest.

270
00:20:18,318 --> 00:20:18,779
[SPEAKER_04]: So be it.

271
00:20:19,159 --> 00:20:21,903
[SPEAKER_04]: So he did and he realized, oh, I actually completed all the work.

272
00:20:22,223 --> 00:20:23,125
[SPEAKER_04]: So he used the handle.

273
00:20:23,485 --> 00:20:24,907
[SPEAKER_04]: He did not stop to check the handle.

274
00:20:25,187 --> 00:20:26,108
[SPEAKER_04]: Am I accelerating this?

275
00:20:26,209 --> 00:20:27,090
[SPEAKER_04]: Am I accelerating this?

276
00:20:27,991 --> 00:20:28,111
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

277
00:20:28,191 --> 00:20:34,139
[SPEAKER_04]: He just used it, used it until his hand went numb and he just laid to sleep and then realized, oh, he wore out the handle.

278
00:20:35,465 --> 00:20:36,665
[SPEAKER_04]: And yeah, then he was an arahant.

279
00:20:36,685 --> 00:20:38,906
[SPEAKER_04]: But even then, he couldn't tell how much he's accelerating.

280
00:20:39,486 --> 00:20:44,728
[SPEAKER_04]: And the acceleration, in this sense, was not his committed sort of meditation technique.

281
00:20:44,868 --> 00:20:49,389
[SPEAKER_04]: It was the sense of shame and lack of accomplishment, which was often found in other suttas as well.

282
00:20:50,010 --> 00:21:00,593
[SPEAKER_04]: When a monk was about to kill himself, because he was not free from sensuality for over 25 years of being in robes, and about to use the knife with a despair and anger and frustration,

283
00:21:01,885 --> 00:21:06,670
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, that made him practice, and take up the practice correctly, and finish the work, and then became an arahant.

284
00:21:07,411 --> 00:21:09,433
[SPEAKER_01]: Terra terrigata, full of that.

285
00:21:09,633 --> 00:21:10,975
[SPEAKER_01]: You're basically seeing the danger.

286
00:21:10,995 --> 00:21:13,157
[SPEAKER_01]: The danger becomes... Look, he had the right view.

287
00:21:13,497 --> 00:21:14,418
[SPEAKER_04]: That's, like, yeah.

288
00:21:14,659 --> 00:21:17,061
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's not a question he didn't even know what the dhamma is.

289
00:21:17,382 --> 00:21:19,584
[SPEAKER_04]: So, and that's why he felt so ashamed.

290
00:21:20,325 --> 00:21:22,787
[SPEAKER_04]: For over 20 years that he's been with the Buddha,

291
00:21:23,548 --> 00:21:25,169
[SPEAKER_04]: He knew what the work was.

292
00:21:25,450 --> 00:21:27,071
[SPEAKER_04]: He was a sotāpanna from the start.

293
00:21:27,431 --> 00:21:36,538
[SPEAKER_04]: Yet, he didn't do it because he was too busy distracting himself with helping these people, helping those people, serving the Buddha and everything else.

294
00:21:36,698 --> 00:21:37,819
[SPEAKER_04]: He didn't do his work.

295
00:21:37,839 --> 00:21:42,183
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, the Buddha himself reprimanded him many times, quite harshly, in the suttas.

296
00:21:43,163 --> 00:21:52,551
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's not just a question of he doesn't have a clue what the practice is and just out of a blue decided he's going to sit and meditate and in the morning he was an arahant.

297
00:21:53,252 --> 00:21:55,633
[SPEAKER_04]: He knew exactly what the practice was, he just wasn't doing it.

298
00:21:55,893 --> 00:21:57,134
[SPEAKER_04]: He was distracting himself from it.

299
00:21:57,714 --> 00:22:02,536
[SPEAKER_00]: Could you say that if you want to accelerate the work, do the work, basically?

300
00:22:02,556 --> 00:22:04,337
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more as in... Well, exactly.

301
00:22:04,797 --> 00:22:10,660
[SPEAKER_04]: There is no magical kind of, I accelerate certain behavior, they will then result in this.

302
00:22:11,280 --> 00:22:11,400
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

303
00:22:11,440 --> 00:22:14,101
[SPEAKER_04]: You accelerate what you know need acceleration.

304
00:22:14,682 --> 00:22:21,605
[SPEAKER_04]: And what needs acceleration is, again, protecting the context, not giving, not maintaining,

305
00:22:22,443 --> 00:22:29,925
[SPEAKER_04]: Not tolerating the mind that is sort of delighting in sensuality, having thoughts of cruelty, having thoughts of conceit and vanity and so on.

306
00:22:30,565 --> 00:22:35,447
[SPEAKER_04]: So, yeah, the work needs to be done and you could do it faster, more efficiently, if the work is clear.

307
00:22:37,207 --> 00:22:39,588
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if you want to accelerate the progress towards arahantship,

308
00:22:40,530 --> 00:22:43,312
[SPEAKER_04]: You need to understand first what progress towards arahantship is.

309
00:22:43,433 --> 00:22:47,696
[SPEAKER_04]: In other words, you need to get the right view, become a sotāpanna, and then you'll know exactly what to do.

310
00:22:47,736 --> 00:22:51,620
[SPEAKER_04]: And if you commit to it, as the Buddha said himself, you might get it in a fortnight.

311
00:22:51,980 --> 00:22:57,445
[SPEAKER_04]: You might clear your mind from any assumption, any resistance towards anything felt within a fortnight.

312
00:22:57,565 --> 00:23:00,107
[SPEAKER_04]: If not, at least within seven years.

313
00:23:00,467 --> 00:23:04,150
[SPEAKER_04]: If there is some residue left, well, again, you wouldn't have to worry.

314
00:23:04,250 --> 00:23:05,592
[SPEAKER_04]: Eventually, it will sort itself out.

315
00:23:06,713 --> 00:23:08,154
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you might maybe say,

316
00:23:09,447 --> 00:23:18,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Clarifying, well, clarifying the work, clarifying the danger that you're in, clarifying the escape from that danger, clarifying, making an effort.

317
00:23:19,295 --> 00:23:34,587
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, but even then, even then, the point is, even then, the relationship between your context, your effort, the danger, the sense restraint, and the mind development, it's not a power of I choose one, two, three, four, five, and this is the result.

318
00:23:34,687 --> 00:23:37,469
[SPEAKER_04]: It always has that indirect certain aspect to it.

319
00:23:39,309 --> 00:23:50,333
[SPEAKER_04]: So, yes, if you really withdraw your elephant from the wilderness, really use the leather straps, really feed it at the right time, right things, it will calm down quicker.

320
00:23:51,653 --> 00:23:57,595
[SPEAKER_04]: But that's not to say that you can influence that quickness directly to make it calm down quicker.

321
00:23:59,355 --> 00:24:06,858
[SPEAKER_04]: Trying to make it calm down quicker would be stopping the work using the axe handle to check is it wearing out.

322
00:24:07,895 --> 00:24:09,777
[SPEAKER_04]: You can't check it while you're using it.

323
00:24:09,817 --> 00:24:11,819
[SPEAKER_04]: So you realize, let me stop checking.

324
00:24:12,700 --> 00:24:14,922
[SPEAKER_04]: Let me just work and use it.

325
00:24:16,323 --> 00:24:21,028
[SPEAKER_04]: When it runs out, I will know it's liberated, as the arahants would say.

326
00:24:21,508 --> 00:24:21,849
[SPEAKER_04]: Or is it?

327
00:24:22,289 --> 00:24:22,630
[SPEAKER_04]: How much?

328
00:24:23,430 --> 00:24:24,972
[SPEAKER_04]: You can't quantify it in that sense.

329
00:24:29,900 --> 00:24:50,197
[SPEAKER_02]: So, basically, Ananda didn't need to really to do any practice, so... well, I mean, any, say, a formal practice, so he could just... he could just have stayed, I know, in his... near the boat and... yeah, whatever, and this could... this would be his meditation.

330
00:24:50,497 --> 00:24:52,679
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, yeah, his meditation is on the level of the mind.

331
00:24:53,430 --> 00:24:58,456
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the meditation is on the level of where the mind inclines, what the assumptions there are.

332
00:24:58,496 --> 00:25:06,004
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the practice is to unravel that, disentangle it, abandon the unwholesome, cultivate the wholesome.

333
00:25:06,825 --> 00:25:10,909
[SPEAKER_04]: So, nobody needs to do any practice in that sense, like physically.

334
00:25:11,531 --> 00:25:16,074
[SPEAKER_04]: What you physically need to do, is withdraw yourself from sensuality, not act out.

335
00:25:16,434 --> 00:25:20,777
[SPEAKER_04]: When you're touched by unpleasant feeling, don't give rise to actions by body and by speech.

336
00:25:21,338 --> 00:25:26,501
[SPEAKER_04]: Cultivate that endurance, being able to take it, so you don't act out of it, so you see what the context is behind it.

337
00:25:27,662 --> 00:25:35,448
[SPEAKER_04]: So, what physically needs to be done, is withdrawn from acts of sensuality, seclusion, moderation in eating.

338
00:25:36,310 --> 00:25:41,773
[SPEAKER_04]: And then not spending all your time sleeping or distracting yourself with work or talking with others.

339
00:25:41,833 --> 00:25:43,934
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the extent of physical methods.

340
00:25:45,555 --> 00:25:50,017
[SPEAKER_04]: Then, the watchfulness, the knowledge of intentions, that's... don't matter what you do physically.

341
00:25:50,477 --> 00:26:03,364
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, the Buddha said he... when he purified his mind, entered the first jhāna, then, whether he was walking, going to the toilet, clearing his bowl, eating, whatever he was doing, it was divine, because the mind was fully turned away from that.

342
00:26:04,304 --> 00:26:05,405
[SPEAKER_04]: He has surmounted it.

343
00:26:09,730 --> 00:26:10,331
[SPEAKER_04]: And now you know.

344
00:26:11,372 --> 00:26:13,756
[SPEAKER_04]: So, this is how I accelerate my practice.

345
00:26:15,338 --> 00:26:18,563
[SPEAKER_04]: By providing the suitable food, avoiding the unsuitable food.

346
00:26:18,964 --> 00:26:24,813
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't know to what degree the speed would increase, but I know for sure it will have to go in that direction.

347
00:26:25,680 --> 00:26:34,283
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the more suitable food it is, the better the withdrawal, the more effort I make into protecting the context, the faster my mind is.

348
00:26:34,683 --> 00:26:39,225
[SPEAKER_04]: Basically, the more I work with an axe handle, the more it is wearing out.

349
00:26:39,665 --> 00:26:40,906
[SPEAKER_04]: Just factually, it's wearing down.

350
00:26:41,826 --> 00:26:46,208
[SPEAKER_04]: If I pick it up once a month, well, that's the acceleration rate, once a month.

351
00:26:47,640 --> 00:26:51,902
[SPEAKER_04]: If I work with it every single day, six hours a day, well, I still...

352
00:26:52,042 --> 00:26:55,823
[SPEAKER_04]: I can't see how much directly, but I know for sure it's more than once a month.

353
00:26:58,004 --> 00:27:06,948
[SPEAKER_04]: And also, another reason why you cannot directly influence or know acceleration is because you don't really know how much that mind needs to be completely purified.

354
00:27:07,583 --> 00:27:11,486
[SPEAKER_04]: So, all you can do is provide a context and then keep working on it.

355
00:27:11,966 --> 00:27:16,830
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's why it says arahants would carry on practicing mindfulness, so to speak, because it ceases to be practice for them.

356
00:27:17,390 --> 00:27:22,434
[SPEAKER_04]: And they would recognize when the mind is liberated and they would know liberated mind as liberated mind.

357
00:27:22,895 --> 00:27:26,517
[SPEAKER_04]: They would know, oh, there is no coming back to what has been liberated from.

358
00:27:27,898 --> 00:27:36,385
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, at that point they can drop the practice, but then you realize, well, there's nothing for them to drop, because they haven't been doing anything in that sense of, oh, I got to stop these duties now.

359
00:27:37,935 --> 00:27:41,918
[SPEAKER_04]: But, yeah, sense of urgency is an acceleration factor.

360
00:27:43,400 --> 00:27:45,081
[SPEAKER_04]: Fear is an acceleration factor.

361
00:27:45,121 --> 00:27:47,963
[SPEAKER_04]: Divine messengers are acceleration factors.

362
00:27:49,965 --> 00:27:59,553
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you want to accelerate your practice, ponder about what you're liable to, what's about to happen to your health, to your life, to the loved ones, to the things you possess.

363
00:28:00,154 --> 00:28:03,537
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, inevitable, inconceivable for it to be otherwise.

364
00:28:04,317 --> 00:28:10,720
[SPEAKER_04]: Yet, by ignoring it, you develop... people develop this assumption that it's not going to happen.

365
00:28:11,320 --> 00:28:12,680
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, no, of course I know it's going to happen.

366
00:28:12,760 --> 00:28:13,621
[SPEAKER_04]: But you don't dwell on it.

367
00:28:13,701 --> 00:28:16,342
[SPEAKER_04]: If you dwell on it, then it will sink in, that it will happen.

368
00:28:16,902 --> 00:28:18,123
[SPEAKER_04]: And that will be felt differently.

369
00:28:18,803 --> 00:28:19,443
[SPEAKER_04]: So, there you go.

370
00:28:19,663 --> 00:28:21,384
[SPEAKER_04]: You're going to have immediate acceleration.

371
00:28:22,353 --> 00:28:27,396
[SPEAKER_04]: But, be careful before you start accelerating, because you better know what direction you need to accelerate to.

372
00:28:28,117 --> 00:28:37,163
[SPEAKER_04]: If you still don't even know what the practice is, and then just start accelerating things, well, I suppose, if you have enough composure, that could make you then really try to find out what the practice is.

373
00:28:37,904 --> 00:28:39,184
[SPEAKER_04]: But it could also make you break down.

374
00:28:39,565 --> 00:28:39,785
[SPEAKER_04]: So...

375
00:28:43,768 --> 00:28:47,230
[SPEAKER_04]: The two types of... two results of being touched by suffering correctly.

376
00:28:48,867 --> 00:28:50,408
[SPEAKER_04]: Noble search or mental derangement?

377
00:28:50,828 --> 00:28:57,032
[SPEAKER_04]: That's exactly what I ended up describing here.

378
00:28:57,052 --> 00:29:13,723
[SPEAKER_05]: Someone said recently it's also about not infinitely trying to clarify the idea of what the practice or what

379
00:29:16,359 --> 00:29:26,905
[SPEAKER_05]: Enlightenment is, but also asking myself, am I applying myself to the degree to which I have understood?

380
00:29:26,945 --> 00:29:27,465
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, yeah.

381
00:29:28,246 --> 00:29:29,166
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, that's that other...

382
00:29:29,186 --> 00:29:30,667
[SPEAKER_04]: I mentioned it, it was yesterday.

383
00:29:31,632 --> 00:29:37,015
[SPEAKER_04]: So, he visits the wise man, a person intent upon his welfare.

384
00:29:37,255 --> 00:29:40,136
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, I better find a way out of this situation.

385
00:29:40,637 --> 00:29:42,618
[SPEAKER_04]: So, he visits the wise man.

386
00:29:42,898 --> 00:29:44,198
[SPEAKER_04]: He doesn't look for faults.

387
00:29:44,259 --> 00:29:45,879
[SPEAKER_04]: It doesn't mean he accepts everything they say.

388
00:29:45,939 --> 00:29:46,240
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

389
00:29:46,420 --> 00:29:49,121
[SPEAKER_04]: He hears the teaching and he reflexively ponders on it.

390
00:29:49,441 --> 00:29:50,882
[SPEAKER_04]: Then, he measures the task.

391
00:29:51,895 --> 00:29:55,698
[SPEAKER_04]: In other words, it's not just a question of accepting the view that I presented to you.

392
00:29:55,798 --> 00:29:57,459
[SPEAKER_04]: It's exactly what you just said.

393
00:29:57,479 --> 00:30:00,701
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, so, to this extent, this is what I do.

394
00:30:01,101 --> 00:30:02,522
[SPEAKER_04]: This is how I apply myself.

395
00:30:02,682 --> 00:30:04,564
[SPEAKER_04]: This is what I don't do.

396
00:30:05,464 --> 00:30:13,670
[SPEAKER_04]: Let me do it and see whether it brings some sort of affirmation of that this is the right practice and so on.

397
00:30:14,030 --> 00:30:16,872
[SPEAKER_04]: But it is, through doing, you can then find out.

398
00:30:16,932 --> 00:30:19,494
[SPEAKER_04]: But not like, oh, this is pleasing to me, thus I accept it.

399
00:30:20,535 --> 00:30:24,461
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't... this is displeasing to me because I don't like how he looks, thus I don't accept it.

400
00:30:25,703 --> 00:30:26,524
[SPEAKER_04]: That's not wisdom.

401
00:30:27,085 --> 00:30:27,726
[SPEAKER_04]: That's just preference.

402
00:30:27,746 --> 00:30:28,688
[SPEAKER_01]: Or let me compare...

403
00:30:29,672 --> 00:30:33,574
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me go to the other wise man, and the other wise man, and the other wise man.

404
00:30:33,594 --> 00:30:35,415
[SPEAKER_01]: You can compare everything and see which one is...

405
00:30:35,515 --> 00:30:39,477
[SPEAKER_04]: If you lack clarity as to what you need to really apply, you can compare.

406
00:30:39,717 --> 00:30:50,883
[SPEAKER_04]: But exactly, then you could equally lose perspective and end up being just revolving around comparisons, and drawing parallels, and similarities, and, oh yeah, the two factors from here respond to five, and this, this, this.

407
00:30:51,604 --> 00:30:52,865
[SPEAKER_04]: Are you applying any of that?

408
00:30:53,865 --> 00:30:59,789
[SPEAKER_04]: If so, is there a measurable kind of freedom from suffering felt, experienced, clarity in regard to...

409
00:30:59,809 --> 00:31:15,901
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, now, if there is no access to information, if there is no access anymore to any sutta, any teaching, any questions and answers, would I know exactly what to do, and if I stick to it, complete freedom from suffering, here and now, within seven days, seven months, seven years?

410
00:31:16,762 --> 00:31:17,723
[SPEAKER_04]: Do I know that?

411
00:31:19,045 --> 00:31:19,606
[SPEAKER_04]: Seven lifetimes.

412
00:31:19,666 --> 00:31:22,067
[SPEAKER_04]: Or seven lifetimes, sure, but you don't necessarily see that.

413
00:31:22,307 --> 00:31:24,289
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, even Sotāpanna is needed to be told that by the Buddha.

414
00:31:24,769 --> 00:31:25,890
[SPEAKER_04]: But this you would see.

415
00:31:26,790 --> 00:31:27,931
[SPEAKER_04]: No, this is the way out.

416
00:31:28,331 --> 00:31:29,212
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm sure already.

417
00:31:30,773 --> 00:31:31,253
[SPEAKER_04]: Do I see it?

418
00:31:31,473 --> 00:31:37,317
[SPEAKER_04]: And if there is no more distractions or other input, if I were to stick to it, would this free me from suffering?

419
00:31:37,357 --> 00:31:38,538
[SPEAKER_04]: Do I have such knowledge?

420
00:31:40,512 --> 00:31:41,713
[SPEAKER_04]: Knowing the way out, basically.

421
00:31:41,993 --> 00:31:42,793
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's sotāpatti.

422
00:31:42,993 --> 00:31:43,633
[SPEAKER_04]: Knowing the way out.

423
00:31:43,733 --> 00:31:50,977
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why it says in the suttas, one who becomes sotāpanna, he's like... he's beyond the instruction from anybody else.

424
00:31:51,537 --> 00:31:55,239
[SPEAKER_04]: He's beyond the changes in the views of the world.

425
00:31:55,679 --> 00:32:02,862
[SPEAKER_04]: And he's certainly beyond following any other teacher, in a sense, even if the entire world says, oh, the Buddha was wrong.

426
00:32:02,982 --> 00:32:04,943
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, he knows that the entire world is wrong.

427
00:32:05,763 --> 00:32:09,805
[SPEAKER_04]: Because on account of understanding what the Buddha instructed, he himself, here and now,

428
00:32:10,887 --> 00:32:12,829
[SPEAKER_04]: Seize the complete freedom from suffering.

429
00:32:13,730 --> 00:32:24,659
[SPEAKER_04]: But then, yeah, you can become negligent, distracted, and so on, because he's kind of free on one level, certainly from Puthujjana's point of view, which is why the complacency can set in.

430
00:32:25,140 --> 00:32:32,846
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's why the Buddha needed to constantly remind the the noble disciples that there is still work to be done, thus do it, because you can.

431
00:32:32,986 --> 00:32:33,527
[SPEAKER_04]: It's right there.

432
00:32:34,586 --> 00:32:43,650
[SPEAKER_04]: Or if some external circumstance prompted them, such as the Buddha's passing away, the despair, the shame, oh, okay, now there's really nothing else worth of my time.

433
00:32:43,910 --> 00:32:45,331
[SPEAKER_04]: See, that's what Ananda felt.

434
00:32:45,431 --> 00:32:49,972
[SPEAKER_04]: He felt serving the Buddha, being around the Buddha was a justifiable distraction.

435
00:32:49,992 --> 00:32:53,774
[SPEAKER_04]: Because as far as the distractions go, it was pretty justifiable.

436
00:32:54,134 --> 00:32:55,335
[SPEAKER_02]: But isn't it a wrong view?

437
00:32:55,955 --> 00:32:56,215
[SPEAKER_02]: Sorry?

438
00:32:57,015 --> 00:32:57,976
[SPEAKER_02]: Isn't it a wrong view?

439
00:32:58,076 --> 00:32:58,956
[SPEAKER_02]: Which one?

440
00:32:59,016 --> 00:33:00,677
[SPEAKER_02]: That there is justifiable destruction.

441
00:33:02,921 --> 00:33:07,344
[SPEAKER_04]: Not necessarily like a wrong view in terms of not knowing what the way out of suffering is.

442
00:33:07,604 --> 00:33:09,025
[SPEAKER_04]: It's certainly an impediment.

443
00:33:09,366 --> 00:33:10,547
[SPEAKER_04]: Distraction is distraction.

444
00:33:11,007 --> 00:33:12,948
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why Sotāpanna is not an arahant.

445
00:33:13,329 --> 00:33:15,611
[SPEAKER_04]: But Sotāpanna doesn't have the wrong views.

446
00:33:15,691 --> 00:33:16,952
[SPEAKER_04]: But he still has the hindrances.

447
00:33:17,452 --> 00:33:18,593
[SPEAKER_04]: He still has the distraction.

448
00:33:18,633 --> 00:33:20,734
[SPEAKER_04]: He still has degree of lust, aversion, conceit.

449
00:33:22,336 --> 00:33:23,617
[SPEAKER_04]: So, I wouldn't say it's a wrong view.

450
00:33:24,137 --> 00:33:28,360
[SPEAKER_04]: I would say it's a... You can have it with the right view that has not been fully developed.

451
00:33:29,041 --> 00:33:30,142
[SPEAKER_04]: Those are the residues.

452
00:33:30,910 --> 00:33:35,153
[SPEAKER_04]: The distraction, the lust, the irritation, the hindrances.

453
00:33:35,873 --> 00:33:41,277
[SPEAKER_04]: Only arahant has destroyed hindrances.

454
00:33:41,717 --> 00:33:45,340
[SPEAKER_02]: But, I mean, it's a false conception.

455
00:33:45,700 --> 00:33:46,040
[SPEAKER_02]: Which one?

456
00:33:46,320 --> 00:33:50,743
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that there is something which is worse of distracting myself.

457
00:33:51,104 --> 00:33:53,265
[SPEAKER_02]: So, basically... No, it's a relative.

458
00:33:53,325 --> 00:33:54,226
[SPEAKER_02]: I wouldn't say it's false.

459
00:33:54,846 --> 00:33:58,549
[SPEAKER_04]: As I said, as far as the distractions go, serving the Buddha,

460
00:33:59,593 --> 00:34:01,594
[SPEAKER_04]: It's least harmful distraction.

461
00:34:02,014 --> 00:34:11,398
[SPEAKER_04]: But yes, from an arahant's point of view, or one who wants to be an arahant, it's a distraction, which is why the Buddha kept telling him off as well, to not distract himself.

462
00:34:13,759 --> 00:34:21,703
[SPEAKER_04]: But if that were to be on the level of the view, then there would be no noble disciples that understood the path, but haven't fully developed.

463
00:34:21,783 --> 00:34:26,545
[SPEAKER_04]: It would be either you have a wrong view, you know nothing, and you have a right view, you are an arahant.

464
00:34:27,107 --> 00:34:30,509
[SPEAKER_04]: But there is the whole area in between, which is often why people can't understand.

465
00:34:31,070 --> 00:34:37,814
[SPEAKER_04]: How can sotāpanna not want to practice for nibbāna, become distracted from it, engage with sensuality?

466
00:34:37,934 --> 00:34:38,595
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, that's how.

467
00:34:39,615 --> 00:34:40,676
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not on the same level.

468
00:34:40,736 --> 00:34:43,298
[SPEAKER_04]: You got the overall view, the freedom, the way out.

469
00:34:43,818 --> 00:34:44,979
[SPEAKER_04]: But now you gotta do it.

470
00:34:45,720 --> 00:34:47,641
[SPEAKER_04]: Now you gotta protect your mind from unwholesome...

471
00:34:47,681 --> 00:34:48,401
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, the Buddha gave...

472
00:34:49,422 --> 00:34:51,984
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't know, maybe not hundreds, but many similes, exactly.

473
00:34:52,004 --> 00:34:54,926
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, I would come and I would pull the arrow out of the wound.

474
00:34:55,506 --> 00:35:03,091
[SPEAKER_04]: The surgeon would come and do that and tell, listen good man, I pulled the arrow, no more poison left, but the wound is still there.

475
00:35:03,671 --> 00:35:05,292
[SPEAKER_04]: It will take time for it to heal.

476
00:35:06,053 --> 00:35:13,778
[SPEAKER_04]: If you want to accelerate its healing, eat the right food, don't eat the wrong food, cover it up, don't expose it to dust and the sun and so on.

477
00:35:14,278 --> 00:35:22,943
[SPEAKER_04]: But then, that man would, eh... expose it to the dust, expose it to the sun, eat the unsuitable food, the wound would get infected and he would suffer on account of it.

478
00:35:23,283 --> 00:35:31,607
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, he gave exact... That's how somebody gets the right view, pulls the arrow of ignorance, the poisonous humor of craving and so on.

479
00:35:32,127 --> 00:35:34,729
[SPEAKER_04]: But factually, the mind still needs time to tame down.

480
00:35:35,109 --> 00:35:39,932
[SPEAKER_04]: But then, they go and do unsuitable things, attend to unsuitable things, distract themselves.

481
00:35:40,452 --> 00:35:42,493
[SPEAKER_04]: So, they perpetuate the wound unnecessarily.

482
00:35:44,360 --> 00:35:45,220
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the difference here.

483
00:35:48,822 --> 00:36:06,349
[SPEAKER_02]: So, can one then say that a sotāpanna has, let's say, right view with regard to the practice, but he might have lots of wrong views regarding, well, maybe even regarding hindrances?

484
00:36:08,029 --> 00:36:08,529
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, no.

485
00:36:08,609 --> 00:36:09,670
[SPEAKER_04]: See, again, it's not...

486
00:36:10,677 --> 00:36:12,058
[SPEAKER_04]: The views you're talking about...

487
00:36:12,418 --> 00:36:15,441
[SPEAKER_04]: So, right view is not on a level of opinion about the practice.

488
00:36:16,341 --> 00:36:18,223
[SPEAKER_04]: Right view is the existential attitude.

489
00:36:18,803 --> 00:36:26,869
[SPEAKER_04]: Now, sure, within an existential attitude of right kind or wrong kind, you can have many irrelevant views.

490
00:36:26,889 --> 00:36:32,834
[SPEAKER_04]: Like Sotāpanna can have many views on other things that have nothing to do with the practice, or they're secondary to it.

491
00:36:35,708 --> 00:36:47,020
[SPEAKER_04]: But fundamentally, his right... the nature of the right view, it means that it's not on the level or the same level as wrong views and opinions or generally ideas that people would have.

492
00:36:47,920 --> 00:36:56,089
[SPEAKER_04]: It's the view that basically has uprooted the ātavāda in regard to any view you might have.

493
00:36:56,149 --> 00:36:58,271
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's not just over there, comparable.

494
00:37:01,563 --> 00:37:05,504
[SPEAKER_03]: So, they might be ignorant about, like, some worldly thing?

495
00:37:05,864 --> 00:37:15,006
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, they might be ignorant about those, like, more detailed aspects of the Dhamma or the Buddha's instruction, because they haven't done it long enough, they haven't pondered it long enough.

496
00:37:15,246 --> 00:37:24,989
[SPEAKER_04]: But they cannot be ignorant about what this is, about the nature of not-self, about where the true cause of suffering is, and where the true escape is.

497
00:37:25,049 --> 00:37:26,349
[SPEAKER_04]: They can't be ignorant about that.

498
00:37:26,869 --> 00:37:35,071
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, Sotāpanna could engage in sensuality, but if he had to ask himself, or look, is this good, beneficial?

499
00:37:35,091 --> 00:37:38,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he wouldn't need to... Yeah, but then he couldn't do it.

500
00:37:38,632 --> 00:37:39,252
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, that's the thing.

501
00:37:39,332 --> 00:37:45,694
[SPEAKER_04]: So, what Sotāpanna can do, is turn a blind eye to his right existential attitude of the right view.

502
00:37:46,594 --> 00:37:55,517
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's like, when people did become Sotāpannas, and then, okay, but, you know, let me go and, you know, home, mother, wife, I have to look after,

503
00:37:56,588 --> 00:38:01,510
[SPEAKER_04]: As in, they are content now with that deeper understanding of where the escape is.

504
00:38:01,530 --> 00:38:06,532
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, see, you can be in the prison, and what is really frightening is you don't know the way out.

505
00:38:06,912 --> 00:38:08,252
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, really, you don't know the way out.

506
00:38:08,993 --> 00:38:10,493
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just you're completely entrapped.

507
00:38:11,133 --> 00:38:12,694
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, you find the way out.

508
00:38:13,741 --> 00:38:15,723
[SPEAKER_04]: You fully... and even the guards are not aware of it.

509
00:38:16,103 --> 00:38:16,823
[SPEAKER_04]: Nobody's aware of it.

510
00:38:17,164 --> 00:38:24,609
[SPEAKER_04]: And you can see it, and you can go halfway up and come back, but you're not leaving the prison yet, because it's going to be a bit unpleasant to crawl through the way out.

511
00:38:25,290 --> 00:38:30,674
[SPEAKER_04]: And also, you got some friends and people you need to help in here, before you go out.

512
00:38:30,694 --> 00:38:32,755
[SPEAKER_04]: Because if I go out, you're on your own.

513
00:38:33,076 --> 00:38:34,197
[SPEAKER_04]: You don't exist for me anymore.

514
00:38:35,217 --> 00:38:37,179
[SPEAKER_04]: But...

515
00:38:37,199 --> 00:38:37,799
[SPEAKER_04]: I want to help them.

516
00:38:38,079 --> 00:38:42,343
[SPEAKER_04]: They helped me here before I knew the way out, or whatever, children, dependents, and so on.

517
00:38:43,275 --> 00:38:45,256
[SPEAKER_04]: So, they postpone the way out.

518
00:38:45,456 --> 00:38:46,136
[SPEAKER_04]: It's going nowhere.

519
00:38:46,357 --> 00:38:48,137
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's another sense of safety where it comes from.

520
00:38:48,157 --> 00:38:51,139
[SPEAKER_04]: Sotāpanna's right view, it's not going anywhere.

521
00:38:52,059 --> 00:38:55,421
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's exactly why they can procrastinate.

522
00:38:56,201 --> 00:38:59,163
[SPEAKER_04]: And the first time it is like, but how can they...

523
00:38:59,902 --> 00:39:05,044
[SPEAKER_04]: Because the idea of enlightenment is just this experience and it's just boom, done, finished, there's nothing in between.

524
00:39:05,625 --> 00:39:12,328
[SPEAKER_04]: But then you have the Buddha constantly reminding monks, sotāpanna, sakadāgāmīs, anāgāmīs, for not completing the work.

525
00:39:12,628 --> 00:39:14,028
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, that's how you not complete the work.

526
00:39:14,609 --> 00:39:19,411
[SPEAKER_04]: You know where the work is, it requires doing, but there is something else keeping you to this.

527
00:39:20,691 --> 00:39:23,973
[SPEAKER_04]: Justifiable, not some ridiculous... Sewing robes.

528
00:39:24,915 --> 00:39:33,820
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, when they were sewing robes and spreading the order and teaching the nuns and all of that, but at the same time, have you escaped all the way out?

529
00:39:34,441 --> 00:39:37,683
[SPEAKER_04]: You got to put that first.

530
00:39:37,783 --> 00:39:40,544
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's exactly what I'm... that's exactly the difference here.

531
00:39:41,985 --> 00:39:52,812
[SPEAKER_04]: And see, now, although a sotāpanna would choose to stay in that prison, there is no more fear, there is no more dread and terror from the guards, from not knowing the escape.

532
00:39:53,635 --> 00:39:54,776
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, oh, you know the escape.

533
00:39:54,836 --> 00:40:00,944
[SPEAKER_04]: And not just that you know the escape, you know that you know the escape, and anything that could block that escape, does not even know about the escape.

534
00:40:02,045 --> 00:40:11,956
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's an internal dimension between you and escape, that nobody, not anything in the world, devas, brahmas, anything, can come in between.

535
00:40:12,757 --> 00:40:15,299
[SPEAKER_04]: So, yeah, it's pretty much quite an immeasurable confidence.

536
00:40:15,979 --> 00:40:19,722
[SPEAKER_04]: But, you do have to start crawling up that escape, eventually.

537
00:40:20,202 --> 00:40:24,285
[SPEAKER_04]: And Ananda did, when there was no more justifiable reasons for him to stay in the prison.

538
00:40:25,006 --> 00:40:27,808
[SPEAKER_04]: Paired with all the shame that he felt on account of Arahants dismissing him.

539
00:40:28,509 --> 00:40:32,171
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, Mahākāśipu was quite blunt, some might say rude.

540
00:40:33,732 --> 00:40:36,414
[SPEAKER_04]: On a number of occasions calling him young boy and stuff like that.

541
00:40:36,454 --> 00:40:38,396
[SPEAKER_04]: He says, when you call me young boy, I have grey hairs.

542
00:40:38,736 --> 00:40:41,318
[SPEAKER_04]: Completely disrespecting him, because he's not doing the work.

543
00:40:42,899 --> 00:40:44,320
[SPEAKER_04]: Not that he doesn't know where the work is.

544
00:40:44,340 --> 00:40:45,820
[SPEAKER_04]: He's just distracting himself.

545
00:40:46,460 --> 00:40:58,505
[SPEAKER_04]: And from somebody's point of view, like Maha Kasepa, who was like the foremost austere determination, jhāna, as it will, hemp robes, rough forest, refusing any comfort.

546
00:40:59,045 --> 00:41:01,366
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, you could see why he was talking the way he was talking.

547
00:41:01,386 --> 00:41:04,187
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's quite a contrast.

548
00:41:05,147 --> 00:41:07,610
[SPEAKER_00]: For him, only just seven days, he says.

549
00:41:07,670 --> 00:41:09,192
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

550
00:41:09,212 --> 00:41:11,354
[SPEAKER_00]: When he got it, that's all.

551
00:41:12,075 --> 00:41:13,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Seven days, he was done.

552
00:41:13,096 --> 00:41:13,517
[SPEAKER_00]: Too long.

553
00:41:15,046 --> 00:41:31,109
[SPEAKER_04]: When the Buddha called him and says, hey, come on, you finish your work, you're old now, like, getting on, like, while living in the rough forest and wearing rough robes and living off poor food, I get plenty of robes, I get plenty of dwellings, come and you can partake in this, because you did the work.

554
00:41:31,249 --> 00:41:39,011
[SPEAKER_04]: He says, no, no, no, whoever can obtain jhānas and will and all these states of mind and will, this is, this is heaven, this is so pleasant.

555
00:41:39,251 --> 00:41:42,172
[SPEAKER_04]: And it would have praised him for practicing for the future generation, not for him.

556
00:41:43,513 --> 00:41:45,099
[SPEAKER_04]: As in, like, it's inspiring.

557
00:41:45,260 --> 00:41:46,344
[SPEAKER_04]: What he's doing, it's inspiring.

